---
/**
 * **The brand lockup, as the supplied artwork — the only place it is rendered** (MUSE-64).
 *
 * Design system §12 forbids, by name, rebuilding the lockup from separate parts. Two
 * spans of Cormorant and Jost positioned by CSS is that construction, and it is what the
 * masthead and the footer both carried: a wordmark that looks deliberate and is not the
 * logo. `test/lockup.test.ts` is the guard, and it reads the markup rather than the
 * rendered page, because the rebuilt version renders perfectly.
 *
 * ## Why an `<Image>` of a raster and not an inline SVG
 *
 * There is no vector. Both files in `logo/` are a base64 PNG inside a mask with ~5.5° of
 * skew — `.svg` by extension only. They cannot be recoloured with `fill` or
 * `currentColor`, will not scale past their raster resolution, and weigh ~85 KB. So the
 * honest options were to ship the raster properly or to redraw it, and a redraw by
 * tracing is inventing brand. This ships the raster properly: trimmed of its transparent
 * canvas, emitted once at `LOCKUP_EMITTED_WIDTH`, content-hashed by Vite, 11.1 KB of
 * WebP with a pixel-exact alpha channel.
 *
 * `quality` is deliberately low and costs nothing. The artwork is pure white — every
 * opaque pixel is `#FFFFFF` and the shape lives entirely in the alpha channel — so lossy
 * RGB compression has no detail to lose, while `alphaQuality: 100` keeps the hairlines
 * exact. Measured: q80/alpha100 is 11,358 bytes at alpha RMSE 0.00 against the
 * full-fidelity resize; raising `quality` to 90 adds 520 bytes and changes nothing.
 *
 * ## Why no theme swap
 *
 * The header and footer bands are `--surface-deep`, which is plum-ink in *both* themes,
 * so the white lockup is correct on both and the variant does not change. §12's
 * `.logo-white` / `.logo-plum` utilities are for a logo on `--surface`, which this is
 * not. One `<img>`, one URL, no flash, no JavaScript — and `test/lockup.test.ts` asserts
 * the absence, because "no theme swap" is a thing that gets added back helpfully.
 *
 * ## Clear space
 *
 * §12: *"minimum equal to the cap height of 'MUSE' on all four sides."* That is a
 * fraction of the drawn size, not a pixel count, so the component derives it from
 * `width` via `lockupClearSpace`. It is `padding` on the wrapper rather than a margin on
 * the image, so it cannot be collapsed by a neighbour's margin — which would make the
 * rule quietly false on one side only.
 *
 * The block-start and inline-start sides are then pulled back flush with a negative
 * margin, and that is not a cancellation. Clear space is a keep-out zone between the
 * artwork and *other content*; the space a container already holds open on its own
 * leading edges is clear space that exists. Without the pull the lockup would hang
 * 31px inside a column whose every other line starts at the edge, which reads as a
 * mistake rather than as a rule being observed. The trailing padding stays real, and it
 * is the side that matters: the next element in flow is pushed clear of the artwork by
 * the full cap height. The caller's job is to place this where the leading edges have
 * at least that much space — in the footer they have 24–48px of gutter against a 31px
 * requirement, which is why `LOCKUP_WIDTH` there is the number it is.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import lockup from '../assets/muse-lockup-white.png';
import { LOCKUP_EMITTED_WIDTH, lockupClearSpace, lockupHeight } from '../lib/lockup';

interface Props {
  /** How wide the lockup is drawn, in CSS pixels. Below §12's 100px minimum this throws. */
  width: number;
  /**
   * The accessible name, or `''` for a lockup whose name is already carried by an
   * ancestor — a link with its own `aria-label`, say. There is no default: an image of
   * the brand name is either naming the brand or repeating a name something else gave,
   * and which one it is depends on where it was placed. See `test/lockup.test.ts`.
   */
  alt: string;
}
const { width, alt } = Astro.props;

const clear = lockupClearSpace(width);
---

<span
  class="lockup"
  style={`--lockup-width:${width}px;--lockup-clear:${clear}px`}
>
  <Image
    src={lockup}
    alt={alt}
    width={LOCKUP_EMITTED_WIDTH}
    height={lockupHeight(LOCKUP_EMITTED_WIDTH)}
    format="webp"
    quality={80}
    loading="eager"
    decoding="async"
  />
</span>

<style>
  .lockup {
    display: block;
    /* §12's clear space, on all four sides, derived from the drawn width. The box is the
       artwork plus its clear space, so anything laid out against this element is outside
       the protected zone by construction rather than by a number somebody chose. */
    padding: var(--lockup-clear);
    /* See the note above: the leading edges borrow the container's own gutter so the
       artwork aligns with the content around it. The trailing sides keep their padding. */
    margin-block-start: calc(-1 * var(--lockup-clear));
    margin-inline-start: calc(-1 * var(--lockup-clear));
    width: max-content;
  }
  .lockup img {
    display: block;
    width: var(--lockup-width);
    /* The emitted width/height attributes reserve the box; `auto` keeps the ratio from
       the attributes rather than restating it, so there is one source for the aspect. */
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
